<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagannath</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jagannath, Lord of the Universe, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deity worshipped as a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, chiefly at his temple in Puri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, together with Balabhadra and Subhadra. The present temple was begun by the Eastern Ganga king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anantavarman-chodaganga">Anantavarman Chodaganga</a> in the 12th century, and the kings of Odisha served as the god's first servants. His annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> festival, the Rath Yatra, draws huge crowds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagannath.jpg" alt="Jagannath" width="320" height="693" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: no bias — קיין אומוויסנדיקע פּרעפֿערענצן — keyn umvisndike preferentsn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rath_Yatra_in_Munich_2020,_Lord_Jagannath_20200725_160839.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Within Odia Hinduism, Jagannath is held to be the supreme god, Purushottama and the Para Brahman. Most Vaishnava Hindus see him as a regional form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, sometimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and some Shaiva and Shakta Hindus regard him as a tantric form of Bhairava. His icon in the temples is a brightly painted, rough-hewn log of neem wood with a square flat head, a large circular face and two big round eyes without eyelids, one said to symbolise the sun and the other the moon. It has no neck, ears or limbs. The wooden images are replaced with new ones at regular intervals, about every 12 years according to one account.</p><p>At the annual Ratha Yatra, held in June or July, the three images are brought out of the sanctum of the Puri temple, placed in temple cars and pulled by volunteers to the Gundicha Temple, about 3 km away. They stay there eight days and return on the ninth. About a million devotees come to Puri to see Jagannath in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, and similar processions are held at Jagannath temples elsewhere in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Accounts of origin differ. The Skanda Purana tells how King Indradyumna of Avanti sought the sapphire deity Nilamadhava and how a neem log floated ashore, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, disguised as an old carpenter, began to carve the images; the king opened the sealed pavilion early and the figures stayed unfinished and without hands or feet. Scholars have proposed Vedic, Buddhist, Jain, Vaishnava and tribal sources, and others call the deity syncretic. The Daitas, hereditary non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> servitors believed to descend from the Sabara tribe, have the first view of each new image.</p><p>The early inscriptions and texts name only a single deity, Purushottama Jagannath. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate inscription of 1198 says the Puri temple was built by the Eastern Ganga king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anantavarman-chodaganga">Anantavarman Chodaganga</a> (1078-1147), originally for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, which has led to arguments that Balabhadra and Subhadra were added later to form a triad. The Vaishnava reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> is said to have visited Puri between 1107 and 1111.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a>, who spent the last 20 years of his life in Puri, encouraged the identification of Jagannath with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, and the deity is widely revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Guru Nanak made a pilgrimage to Puri in 1506 or 1508. Court historians record that Firuz Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and desecrated the temple, and attacks on the Puri temple became serious after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, under whom the tradition had flourished. A. C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada selected Jagannath for ISKCON temples, which now hold the Ratha Yatra in many Western cities. Early British observers rendered the name as juggernaut, a word that came to mean a very large and unstoppable force.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jagannath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many poets sang of a particular image in a particular temple: Ranganatha at Srirangam, Vithoba at Pandharpur, <b>Jagannath</b> at Puri.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bhoi rulers continued the Gajapati title and the royal role in the cult of <b>Jagannath</b> at Puri, but faced constant revolts by nobles and pressure from the Golconda Sultanate and the Afghan rulers of Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhoi-dynasty">Bhoi dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Anantavarman Chodaganga, who ruled from 1078 to 1147, united the land from the Ganges to the Godavari, moved the capital to Cuttack and began the <b>Jagannath</b> temple at Puri, which became the centre of the state cult.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings ruled as servants of <b>Jagannath</b> of Puri, whose temple was the centre of state ritual.</blockquote><a href="/state/gajapati-kingdom">Gajapati Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khurda rajas were acknowledged as the first servants of <b>Jagannath</b> at Puri, a role that gave them prestige across Odisha.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-khurda">Kingdom of Khurda</a></li></ul></section>
